ce conditions, and the same access path — splitting them\n  would create two epics building the same surface plus an artificial\n  serialization edge. They are captured as scope and acceptance criteria of\n  the single Dashboard epic.\n- **docs-authoring priority exception.** E003 derives P1 from the strategy\n  frontmatter priority (Major → P1); it does not push priority onto E001/E002.\n- **Component mappings.** Strategy lists \"Dashboard\" and \"KServe\" — both are\n  verbatim canonical component names. HLR-5 implies the canonical\n  \"Documentation\" component for E003 (not listed in the strategy's Affected\n  Components; noted below as a health warning). No non-obvious mappings were\n  needed.\n- **Out of scope honored.** Alerting/notifications, status history/timeline\n  views, and ModelMesh deployments are excluded from every epic's scope.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements section. Owning teams were inferred from the\n  architecture context (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`):\n  Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard, KServe → RHOAI Serving. Epic acceptance\n  criteria were derived from the HLRs and problem statement rather than from\n  strategy ACs.\n- **Affected Components list is incomplete.** HLR-5 requires product\n  documentation, but \"Documentation\" is not listed among affected components.\n  The decomposition adds the Documentation epic to guarantee HLR coverage.\n- **HLR-3 freshness target is a soft NFR.** \"Within 30 seconds without a\n  manual reload\" is the only timing requirement; the mechanism (polling\n  interval vs. Kubernetes watch) is left to the implementing team, per the\n  decompose-don't-design constraint.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; all work is\nin-platform.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm