{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9161f0d6f49338eb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9001-E001\n3\ttitle: Surface InferenceService deployment status in the Dashboard model serving views\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9001\n5\tcomponent: Dashboard\n6\tteam: RHOAI Dashboard\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Surface InferenceService deployment status in the Dashboard model serving views\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers deployment status visibility for KServe InferenceService\n34\tmodels directly in the RHOAI Dashboard, so data scientists can tell whether a\n35\tdeployment is healthy without leaving the console or using `oc` commands. It\n36\tcovers the status display in the model serving views, a status detail view\n37\twith underlying condition messages and recent events, automatic status\n38\tfreshness, and filter/sort by status.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Display a deployment status — Ready, Progressing, Failed — for each deployed\n43\t  KServe InferenceService model in the Dashboard model serving views, derived\n44\t  from the InferenceService CR status conditions.\n45\t- Add a status detail view showing the underlying condition messages and\n46\t  recent Kubernetes events for a failed or progressing deployment.\n47\t- Ensure the displayed status reflects the actual InferenceService state\n48\t  within 30 seconds of a change, without a manual page reload. The refresh\n49\t  mechanism (polling interval vs. Kubernetes watch) is an implementation\n50\t  decision for the Dashboard team.\n51\t- Add filter and sort controls for the deployed models list by status.\n52\t- Scope is limited to KServe InferenceService deployments; ModelMesh\n53\t  deployments, alerting/notifications on status transitions, and status\n54\t  history / timeline views are out of scope per the strategy.\n55\t\n56\t## Acceptance Criteria\n57\t\n58\t- Each deployed KServe InferenceService shown in the Dashboard model serving\n59\t  views displays one of the statuses Ready, Progressing, or Failed, derived\n60\t  from the InferenceService status conditions.\n61\t- A user can open a status detail view for a failed or progressing deployment\n62\t  that shows the underlying condition messages and recent events.\n63\t- After a change in the actual InferenceService state, the displayed status\n64\t  reflects the new state within 30 seconds without a manual page reload.\n65\t- The deployed models list can be filtered by status and sorted by status.\n66\t- The condition-to-status mapping used by the Dashboard has been validated\n67\t  with the RHOAI Serving team against actual InferenceService conditions in\n68\t  both serverless and raw deployment modes.\n69\t- The Dashboard build and test pipeline passes with these changes.\n70\t\n71\t## HLR Traceability\n72\t\n73\t- HLR-1 (P0): Users see a deployment status for each deployed model in the\n74\t  Dashboard model serving views.\n75\t- HLR-2 (P0): Users can open a status detail view showing the underlying\n76\t  condition messages and recent events for a failed or progressing deployment.\n77\t- HLR-3 (P1): Displayed status reflects the actual InferenceService state\n78\t  within 30 seconds of a change, without a manual page reload.\n79\t- HLR-4 (P2): Users can filter and sort the deployed models list by status.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b74eb13bf552a6d5","agent_id":"a4b5fc06f5d6c7