on-to-status mapping is a review/AC gate on E001, not a DAG dependency. |\n42\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; documentation describes the status surface that was built. |\n43\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; the documented status semantics must reflect the validated condition mapping. |\n44\t\n45\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n46\t\n47\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n48\t|-----|----------|---------|\n49\t| HLR-1 (status shown per deployed model) | P0 | E001 (implementation), E002 (validation of the condition source) |\n50\t| HLR-2 (status detail view: conditions + events) | P0 | E001 (implementation), E002 (validation of the condition source) |\n51\t| HLR-3 (status fresh within 30s, no reload) | P1 | E001 |\n52\t| HLR-4 (filter/sort by status) | P2 | E001 |\n53\t| HLR-5 (product documentation for statuses and troubleshooting) | P2 | E003 |\n54\t\n55\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n56\t\n57\t## Decomposition Decisions\n58\t\n59\t- **KServe is a passive component.** The strategy states KServe already\n60\t  exposes the needed InferenceService status conditions and open questions\n61\t  are \"None blocking\", so no KServe code changes are in scope. Because the\n62\t  passive component is owned by RHOAI Serving (a different team than the\n63\t  active Dashboard epic), the passive-component validation is a separate\n64\t  validation Implementation epic owned by RHOAI Serving (E002) rather than an\n65\t  AC on the Dashboard epic.\n66\t- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy explicitly declares no blocking\n67\t  open questions, and no conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks tables\n68\t  appear. The refresh mechanism for HLR-3 (polling vs. watch) is an\n69\t  implementation detail the Dashboard team resolves; it does not change which\n70\t  epics exist.\n71\t- **HLR-1 through HLR-4 stay bundled in E001.** HLR-3 (freshness) and HLR-4\n72\t  (filter/sort) are lower-priority HLRs, but they share the same UI surface\n73\t  (model serving views / deployed models list), the same status data derived\n74\t  from InferenceService conditions, and the same access path — splitting them\n75\t  would create two epics building the same surface plus an artificial\n76\t  serialization edge. They are captured as scope and acceptance criteria of\n77\t  the single Dashboard epic.\n78\t- **docs-authoring priority exception.** E003 derives P1 from the strategy\n79\t  frontmatter priority (Major → P1); it does not push priority onto E001/E002.\n80\t- **Component mappings.** Strategy lists \"Dashboard\" and \"KServe\" — both are\n81\t  verbatim canonical component names. HLR-5 implies the canonical\n82\t  \"Documentation\" component for E003 (not listed in the strategy's Affected\n83\t  Components; noted below as a health warning). No non-obvious mappings were\n84\t  needed.\n85\t- **Out of scope honored.** Alerting/notifications, status history/timeline\n86\t  views, and ModelMesh deployments are excluded from every epic's scope.\n87\t\n88\t## Health Warnings\n89\t\n90\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n91\t  Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n92\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Owning teams were inferred from the\n93\t  architecture context (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`):\n94\t  Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard, KServe → RHOAI Serving. Epic acceptance\n95\t  criteria were derived from the HLRs and problem statement rather than from\n96\t  strategy ACs.\n97\t- **Affected Components list is incomplete.** HLR-5 requires product\n98\t  documentation, but \"Documentation\" is not listed among affected components.\n99\t  The decomposition adds the Documentation epic to guarantee HLR coverage.\n100\t- **HLR-3 freshness target is a soft NFR.** \"Within 30 seconds without a\n101\t  manual reload\" is the only timing