{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d8f4ce36f7e46ae","type":"tool_result","content":"skills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:32:- **Rule 11 (docs-authoring not blocked by an Implementation epic)**: add the missing epic to the docs epic's `dependencies` (and to the summary's DAG diagram + justification).\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:43:This deterministically checks the mechanically decidable rules an LLM review demonstrably misses: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance. Treat its output as ground truth over your own reading of the frontmatter — a reviewer asserting \"epic X blocks no P0 work\" without having enumerated every P0 epic's `dependencies` list is exactly the failure this script exists to catch.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:48:- Rule 11 (docs-authoring epic not blocked by an Implementation epic) → severity critical, criterion \"DAG Coherence\" (missing edge)\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:75:Check: Trace the dependency graph. Verify each edge against the DAG construction rules (boundary rules 1-3, investigation edges 4-5, implementation type ordering 6-12, implementation edges 13-16, external dependency edges 17-19, generation rules 20-23, AC rules 24-26). Check that parallel-eligible work (different repos, no shared artifacts) is not unnecessarily serialized. **Implementation-type parallelism (rules 7, 8, 10) — enumerate and check every pair:** epics whose `implementation_type` pairing is mandated parallel must NOT have a blocking edge between them in either direction. Check **every** `konflux-onboarding` epic against **every** general-implementation epic (a decomposition often has more than one general epic — e.g. a component controller AND an OLM/CSV packaging epic; check all of them, not just the most obvious one). Specifically, a `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (rule 10), `license-validation → repo-onboarding` (rule 7), or `license-validation → konflux-onboarding` (rule 8) is a **critical** issue — score this criterion 0. The only permitted coupling between a `konflux-onboarding` epic and its component's general implementation is the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion, never a `dependencies`/DAG edge. Do not accept a rationale that the code \"needs the pipeline first.\" **In particular, an edge justified only by an image digest / SHA / `RELATED_IMAGE` / endpoint URL is a configuration reference (Rule 13), which must be an AC gate, not a `dependencies` edge — flag it as critical, do not accept it as an \"artifact dependency.\"** **Conditional branch files (`{ID}-BRANCH-<letter>-E*.md`), when present:** each MUST set both `branch` (the outcome letter, matching the filename) and `gated_by` (the main-plan Investigation `epic_id` that selects it) — a branch file missing either is a **critical** issue because the pipeline silently drops it. And no main-plan epic may list a branch epic in its `dependencies`: a main-plan epic depending on a `-BRANCH-` node (e.g. main-plan `E004` → `BRANCH-A-E003`) references an epic that is never created as an issue, a **critical** issue — work that hinges on the outcome must depend on the Investigation epic itself, and work that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch. Note: Rule 11 edges (all implementations → `docs-authoring`) are valid DAG edges but do not trigger priority inheritance — do not flag them as unjustified serialization or priority inheritance violations. Verify critical path length against strategy size heuristics (S: 1-2, M standard: 3-4, M with new component: 4-5, L: 5-7). **Cross-check consistency**: The DAG diagram convention is `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\"). Verify that every edge in the decomposition summary DAG diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. If arrows are drawn in the opposite direction (dependent → dependency), flag as a critical issue — the diagram is misleading even if frontmatter is correct. Any mismatch (edge in diagram but not in frontmatter, or frontmatter dependency referencing a nonexistent epic) is a critical issue — score 0. **Cross-check completeness**: also scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:117:Check: Compare the strategy's scope, acceptance criteria, and capabilities against the combined epic set. Look for gaps. Also check cross-epic consistency: when an upstream epic's scope covers multiple items (modules, components, APIs), verify that the downstream epic set collectively accounts for all of them — not silently dropped. Verify that strategy-level context relevant to an epic's scope (risks, assumptions, open questions, stakeholder commitments, etc.) is carried forward — not silently dropped. **Docs-authoring check**: if the strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates section lists `Documentation Support: Yes`, the epic set must include a `docs-authoring` epic (Rule 11). A missing docs-authoring epic when documentation support is required is a major issue — score 0.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:106:### Epic Boundary Rules\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:123:10. `konflux-onboarding` ∥ general implementation parallel (config independent of code; AC gates first execution). **Add NO blocking edge in either direction.** It is tempting to make the general implementation depend on `konflux-onboarding` (\"the code needs a build pipeline first\") — do not. That coupling is enforced by the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC on the general implementation epic, never by a DAG dependency. A `konflux-onboarding → general` edge serializes work that must run in parallel and is a rule violation.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:128:13. Implementation producing artifact another epic's code builds against (API, CRD, library) → consuming Implementation serial. Does NOT apply to configuration references (image digests, endpoint URLs) — those are AC gates. **In particular, an image digest / SHA consumed by OLM/CSV packaging (`RELATED_IMAGE`) is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact: the packaging epic gets a \"pinned image digest available\" AC, NOT a `dependencies` edge on the image-build or `konflux-onboarding` epic.** Wiring such an edge both mis-serializes parallel work and violates Rule 10 — the digest is filled in at execution time, so the epics run in parallel.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:137:### Epic Generation Rules\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:143:### Acceptance Criteria Rules\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:342:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), and priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a4d67d012daeaa9","agent_id":"a2350addcb37507ed","session_id":"d9a0c1a1-801a-4eeb-bfda-a0400f18f850","uuid":"8d32ebb4-357a-4ab6-8866-510cc0abaa5a","timestamp":"2026-10-22T